From c2f6e0a327ced382351c4443242a508b28cb990f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tserdeiro <tserdeiro@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 07:56:08 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(git): gate optional commit hooks on their own auto_commit
 key

A fresh project registers all sixteen optional git.commit hooks
unconditionally in .specify/extensions.yml. auto_commit.default ships
false and every event's own auto_commit.<event>.enabled ships false
too (config-template.yml), so every one of those hooks is still
announced by core commands as if it might do something -- agreeing to
run the announced /speckit.git.commit is a no-op, since the command
itself checks the same disabled config before deciding not to commit.

Give each git.commit hook the condition it already needs to respect at
run time: condition: "config.auto_commit.<event>.enabled == 'true'",
using HookExecutor's existing config.<path> == 'value' grammar.
HookExecutor.should_execute_hook already evaluates this correctly
against the extension's own git-config.yml, live -- turning an event's
auto-commit on later needs no reinstall, since nothing about the
registered hook entry itself has to change. The two mandatory hooks
(git.initialize, git.feature) are untouched; they carry no auto_commit
key to gate on.

Scope, stated plainly: current core command templates do not evaluate
hook conditions -- docs/reference/extensions.md documents that a hook
with a non-empty condition is skipped, unevaluated, and nothing in
src/specify_cli calls should_execute_hook in production. So, on its
own, this change stops all sixteen hooks from being offered through the
stock templates, including for a project that has already enabled an
event's auto-commit today. It is the first half of the fix: the second
half is the templates (or the dispatcher) evaluating a hook's condition
before offering it, which this commit deliberately does not attempt.
Two further limits of the expression: the grammar has no disjunction,
so auto_commit.default's "enable for all, then override" shorthand is
not honored (only the per-event key is), and a mistyped condition
degrades silently to never-executable.

Tests: the manifest declares the expected condition on every git.commit
entry and none on the two mandatory hooks; installing the extension
writes the condition into the registered before_plan entry, and
should_execute_hook is false under the shipped git-config.yml and true
once auto_commit.before_plan.enabled is set to true in it.
